  "text": "This orders table is cheap to build and quick to query. It's also wrong: twenty-four rows, and three of the order IDs appear twice. Nothing errors. Revenue is three hundred thirty-five dollars too high, and every dashboard that reads the table agrees on it. An analytics engineer is the last line of defense before a dashboard, so interviews probe how you catch that first: tests, contracts, freshness, and checks on every change. Nineteen questions. Ten are worked through, most on real rows in Postgres, and nine come quickly at the end.\n\nQuestion one. What does it mean that a dbt test asserts? A test writes an assumption down as a query for the rows that break it. Zero rows back, and the test passes. Any rows back are the failures, and the run fails with them. It never changes the data. It stops the pipeline loudly, instead of letting bad rows reach a dashboard quietly. Question two. What does a failing uniqueness test actually surface? The rows that break it. A unique test on the order ID compiles to roughly this: group by the key, and keep any key that appears more than once. On the page's twenty-four rows, it returns three: orders two, eleven and nineteen, twice each. Those rows are the work. dbt hasn't removed them, and they stay until whatever sent them twice is fixed.",
